## Problem 1 [50 pts], Tests of Asset Pricing Models

**a.** Write a function `Q1_KenFrenchData()` that requires no arguments and returns two objects: **[10 pts]**

- `factors`: a DataFrame containing six columns of monthly data from January 1980 to December 2021, in the following order, drawn from Prof. Ken French's Data Library:
  - the five factors from `F-F_Research_Data_5_Factors_2x3` (Mkt-RF, SMB, HML, RMW, CMA)
  - the momentum factor from `F-F_Momentum_Factor`
- `portfolios`: a DataFrame containing monthly returns on the 17 Industry Portfolios

Make sure that all series in both `factors` and `portfolios` are excess returns (i.e. net of the risk-free rate). You may use `pandas_datareader` or any other method. Report and interpret the first five rows and descriptive statistics of each object.

**b.** Provide descriptive statistics and appropriate graphs for your data. **[10 pts]**

Your graphs must include at minimum: (i) a time-series plot of all six factors; (ii) a time-series plot of the 17 industry portfolio excess returns; (iii) a correlation heatmap of the factors. Make sure all graphs are clearly labelled. In your discussion, address: which factors are most volatile? Which industry portfolios have the highest and lowest mean excess returns? What does the correlation structure of the factors suggest about potential multicollinearity in the asset pricing regressions?

**c.** Write a function `Q1_AssetPricingModel()` that accepts: **[20 pts]**

- a DataFrame `Factors` ($T \times K$) of factor returns
- a DataFrame `Assets` ($T \times N$) of excess returns on assets or portfolios
- a boolean `Traded` indicating whether the factors are excess returns on traded assets

The function estimates the linear asset pricing model in two passes:

- **(i) Time-series pass:** For each asset $i = 1, \ldots, N$, regress $r_{it}$ on a constant and the $K$ factors:

$$
r_{it} = \alpha_i + \boldsymbol{\beta}_i' \mathbf{F}_t + \varepsilon_{it}
$$

  to obtain estimated loadings $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_i$ and intercepts $\hat{\alpha}_i$.

- **(ii) Cross-sectional pass:** Regress the $N$ time-series mean excess returns $\bar{r}_i$ on the estimated loadings $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_i$ to obtain risk premia $\hat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}$.

The function prints a summary of results and returns:

- a $K \times 1$ array of estimated risk premia $\hat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}$
- a $K \times 1$ array of their heteroscedasticity-robust $t$-statistics
- an $N \times K$ array of estimated factor loadings $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}$
- an $N \times K$ array of their heteroscedasticity-robust $t$-statistics
- a scalar $R^2$ from the cross-sectional regression
- a $3 \times 1$ array containing: the $J$-test statistic, its $p$-value, and the degrees of freedom of its null distribution, for $H_0$ that the model adequately explains all cross-sectional differences in expected excess returns

Apply your function to the data from part (a) with `Traded=True` and `Traded=False`. Report and interpret the results from both calls.

**d.** Use your results from part (c) to answer each of the following questions in 2–3 sentences. **[10 pts]**

- Which factors (if any) earn a statistically significant risk premium when we treat the factors as excess returns on traded assets?
- Does the $J$-test suggest the model is well-specified, or do some portfolios command a premium unexplained by the six factors?
- Why does the null distribution of the $J$-test statistic differ depending on whether `Traded` is `True` or `False`? Provide the economic intuition.
- Given that all six factors (Mkt-RF, SMB, HML, RMW, CMA, Mom) are excess returns on self-financing traded portfolios, which version of the model — `Traded=True` or `Traded=False` — is theoretically appropriate here, and why?

